nt of “Write tests first” and “Fail fast.”\n\n**Testing 101**\n\nMaya began to read up on different testing styles: unit testing to ensure individual methods did what they claimed, integration testing to test the collaboration between subsystems, e2e tests to validate the application flow. She learned how tests had an inherent *mutation effect*. That is, when a function’s contract changed, the tests that “replayed” its expected behavior flagged the change. In this way, tests became not just a safety measure but a guardian for future modifications.\n\nHer own first unit test went something like this:\n\n```go\nfunc TestAddUser(t *testing.T) {\n  user := NewUser(\"alice\", \"alice@example.com\")\n  if user.Email != \"alice@example.com\" {\n    t.Errorf(\"expected %s, got %s\", \"alice@example.com\", user.Email)\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe line “If tests fail, don’t merge.” became a motto engraved into her head like a personal spell. Each commit was a small promise that her code would pass every test. She spent longer hours, but she discovered the process felt lighter: *bugs became part of her development rhythm.*\n\n**The Rewind**\n\nA year passed, and a company that was known for \"fast releases with fewer bugs\" made its debut on the Fortune 500 list. The sales department spoke in whispered admiration of how CodeCove delivered on the next product release without a single customer support ticket. The marketing team didn’t just boast about the time to market; they highlighted “zero customer complaints for six months, thanks to a proactive testing culture.”\n\nIt was not all glinting. The CTO had to do a major migration to a new cloud provider. The integration tests became her safety harness, and no surprise issues appeared. When a “security patch” needed to be applied to one of the micro‑services, the suite of tests caught a dependency failure before the code hit the production cluster.\n\nIt was in these moments, under the humming of data centers and the occasional ping of a